\tag{public}
\title{Page replacement}

\p{Once swap space is in play, the OS must decide \em{which} pages to evict from physical memory. The mechanism is the [[CXFM]] machinery; the question of policy is what this hub covers.}

\transclude{CXFM}
\transclude{7WG2}
\transclude{LQV3}

\p{Two refinements make eviction practical on real workloads. A pure global policy lets one greedy process hog memory, which [[HF6H]] fixes with a per-process resident-set cap; and writing victims back one page at a time wastes disk bandwidth, which [[YMHF]] amortizes by batching the dirty list.}

\transclude{HF6H}
\transclude{DEJB}
\transclude{PW7Q}
\transclude{YMHF}

\remark{A common convention is to map the kernel into every process's address space (via [[DEJB]]) so kernel code and structures appear at the same virtual addresses everywhere.}
